#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# doctor - Agora - unit 001, D-019 rider (b).
#
# THIS IS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A GATE. It never produces an "N checks - M
# failures" line (that phrase is reserved for tests/bin/gate-a-wave1.sh and
# tests/bin/gate-a-wave3.sh) and its output must never be quoted as a gate
# verdict. Its job is narrower and comes first: decide whether THIS host can
# do useful work at all, before any invariant or gate runs on it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (D-019). [andres] works from a Windows PC at home and a
# MacBook away from it, and asked for the project to work on both. D-019
# answered: the gate's toolchain lives in a container, so neither host's
# tools are the gate's tools - but host mode stays available as a fallback,
# because a laptop away from home should not need Docker running to do
# useful work. This script is what makes that safe. It runs before any work
# in a session and is meant to become the first step of /retomar (that
# wiring is a separate change; this file does not touch /retomar).
#
# ALL FIVE false greens of unit 001 came from the HOST, not from Agora's own
# code (D-019 evidence list). This script exists so the sixth is caught in
# the first ten seconds instead of two days later:
#   - python3 resolving to the Microsoft Store stub (I-026)
#   - jq/python3 emitting CRLF on stdout (I-025)
#   - GNU grep 3.0 aborting on `-F` combined with `-i` (I-027)
#   - host Python defaulting to cp1252 and dying on non-ASCII bytes (T-320c)
#   - no jq, no composer at all - the gate could not even be evaluated
#
# PHILOSOPHY (matches tests/bin/gate-a-wave3.sh on purpose - one house
# style): report everything it can rather than aborting at the first
# problem. A tool that is missing does not stop the scan of the other
# seventeen; it is recorded, with its fix, and the scan continues. `set -u`,
# no `set -e`.
#
# HYGIENE (T-316/T-321 - do not reintroduce what those tasks had to clean
# up): no counter here is a `grep -c` feeding a comparison - counts are
# either plain integers incremented in control flow (TOOLS_MISSING,
# TOOLS_BROKEN, HAZ_TOTAL, HAZ_PRESENT: incapable of being blank by
# construction, per I-031's preferred fix) or `wc -l` over output this
# script generated itself. Every grep this script runs for its OWN
# bookkeeping avoids `-F` combined with `-i` (the one combination I-027
# proved aborts on this host's grep) - the one place that exact combination
# IS used is the hazard probe that deliberately reproduces I-027, where an
# abort is the expected, correctly-reported outcome, not a bug.
#
# CERTIFIED-PLATFORM DATA. D-019 rider (c): a platform whose dirty-case
# matrix (T-312) has not been run is NOT CERTIFIED, and a result from it must
# be reported as such - loudly, but as a WARNING, never as a failure: host
# mode on an uncertified platform is still explicitly permitted (rider c).
# The task that requested this script asked for that record to live "in a
# versioned data file... your call where" while ALSO restricting this change
# to one new file, tests/bin/doctor, touching nothing else. Both are honored
# by keeping the record HERE, as the clearly-delimited, non-executable block
# immediately below (CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS) rather than folding it into the
# procedural logic further down: certifying a new platform is still a
# one-line, reviewable diff, and no second file exists that could drift out
# of sync with this one. Flagged in the delivery report as a judgment call,
# for [andres]/orquestador to correct if a separate file was actually meant.
#
# TOOL LIST. Six tools are named directly by the task that requested this
# script: grep, jq, python3, curl, git, composer. The rest were SURVEYED, not
# guessed: every tests/bin/* invariant and both gate runners were grepped for
# real invocations (as opposed to the tool's name merely appearing in a
# comment or a filename like composer.json) on 2026-08-22. That survey found
# sed, tr, cut, sort, wc, find, tar, date, mktemp, dirname and basename in
# real use, plus head (no-patches, no-unstable-deps, sbom-check). `xargs`
# appears only in a gate-a-wave1.sh comment describing what T-316 removed,
# not in live code.
#
# TWO CORRECTIONS TO THAT SURVEY, both re-measured 2026-08-25 (T-803), both
# recorded here rather than left standing, because a diagnostic that prints a
# false statement about the toolchain is the exact hazard it exists to catch:
#
#   - `awk` was surveyed as unused and no longer is. tests/bin/config-inventory
#     (T-601, added after the survey) invokes it SEVEN times. It is now probed
#     as a required tool, in group 3, and its character-versus-byte behaviour
#     is probed as a hazard in group 4 - the gate container's awk is mawk,
#     which counts bytes where a character-based rule counts characters.
#
#   - A checksum tool is still not used by anything in THIS repository, and
#     that is the whole reason group 3b exists rather than a report_tool call.
#     drupal/agora_theme's tests/bin/shared-invariants is built entirely out of
#     sha256 comparisons, this script is the host probe for BOTH repositories,
#     and macOS ships no `sha256sum` at all - it ships `shasum`. So the hash
#     tool is REPORTED, never silently selected, and its absence is a note
#     rather than a failure. A check whose inputs differ from the gate's,
#     silently, is worse than one that is noisy (I-054).
#
#
# House rules for tests/bin/ (T-321(c)): `wc -l` never `grep -c` for a compared
# value; numeric guards defaulted ONLY where zero means FAIL; grep rc >= 2 is an
# error, never "no match"; never `-F` with `-i`. The CANONICAL copy of the block,
# with the reasoning and the I-031 asymmetry spelled out, lives in the header of
# tests/bin/no-ci-allow-dev. Read it before adding a counter here.
#
# Usage: tests/bin/doctor   (run from anywhere; it cd's to the repo root)

set -u
# No `set -e`: a missing or broken tool must be recorded, not abort the run.

# ============================================================================
# CERTIFIED PLATFORMS -- versioned data, not logic. To certify a new
# platform, edit ONE line below (or add one) and say so in the commit that
# also records its dirty-case matrix (T-312) run. Nothing else in this file
# needs to change.
#
# format:  <platform-family>|<label>|<yes/no>|<matrix date, or ->|<task, or ->
# ============================================================================
CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS='
windows|Windows (MSYS2 / Git for Windows)|yes|2026-08-22|T-312
macos|macOS|no|-|-
linux|Linux|no|-|-
'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- repo root --
SCRIPT_DIR=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
# shellcheck disable=SC1007
REPO_ROOT=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/../.." && pwd)
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || { echo "FATAL: cannot cd to repo root $REPO_ROOT"; exit 1; }

C_OK=""; C_BAD=""; C_WARN=""; C_OFF=""; C_DIM=""
if [ -t 1 ]; then
  C_OK=$'\033[32m'; C_BAD=$'\033[31m'; C_WARN=$'\033[33m'; C_OFF=$'\033[0m'; C_DIM=$'\033[2m'
fi

N=0
M=0
TOOLS_MISSING=0
TOOLS_BROKEN=0
HAZ_TOTAL=0
HAZ_PRESENT=0

# Truncate a value so the table stays aligned; never hide the fact it was cut.
trunc() {
  _v=$1; _max=$2
  if [ "${#_v}" -gt "$_max" ]; then
    printf '%s...' "$(printf '%s' "$_v" | cut -c1-$((_max - 3)))"
  else
    printf '%s' "$_v"
  fi
}

# check <label> <obtained> <expected>  -- exact string match. Same contract
# as tests/bin/gate-a-wave3.sh's check(): N/M are internal bookkeeping for
# this row's OK/FAIL colour only, never printed as a gate-style summary.
check() {
  _label=$1; _got=$2; _exp=$3
  N=$((N + 1))
  if [ "$_got" = "$_exp" ]; then
    _res="${C_OK}OK${C_OFF}"
  else
    _res="${C_BAD}FAIL${C_OFF}"
    M=$((M + 1))
  fi
  printf '  %-46s %-24s | %-24s | %s\n' \
    "$_label" "$(trunc "$_got" 24)" "$(trunc "$_exp" 24)" "$_res"
}

group() {
  printf '\n%s\n' "$1"
  printf '  %-46s %-24s | %-24s | %s\n' "check" "obtained" "expected" "verdict"
  printf '  %s\n' "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------"
}

note() { printf '  %s%s%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$1" "$C_OFF"; }

# hazard <label> <PRESENT|ABSENT> <consequence-if-present> <source>
# Deliberately NOT check(): a hazard being present is not a failure of this
# script or of the toolchain - it is a fact the invariants already work
# around (see the cited idiom/task). PRESENT is coloured as a warning, never
# as red FAIL, and never counted toward TOOLS_MISSING/TOOLS_BROKEN.
hazard() {
  _label=$1; _state=$2; _cons=$3; _src=$4
  HAZ_TOTAL=$((HAZ_TOTAL + 1))
  if [ "$_state" = PRESENT ]; then
    HAZ_PRESENT=$((HAZ_PRESENT + 1))
    _res="${C_WARN}PRESENT${C_OFF}"
  else
    _res="${C_OK}ABSENT${C_OFF}"
  fi
  printf '  %-46s %-24s | %-24s | %s\n' "$_label" "$(trunc "$_src" 24)" "$(trunc "$_cons" 24)" "$_res"
  note "$_cons"
}

# hazard_skipped <label> <reason> -- the probe's own prerequisite tool is
# unusable, so the probe cannot run. Counted in HAZ_TOTAL (honesty about
# scope) but never in HAZ_PRESENT (we did not confirm anything either way).
hazard_skipped() {
  _label=$1; _reason=$2
  HAZ_TOTAL=$((HAZ_TOTAL + 1))
  printf '  %-46s %-24s | %-24s | %s\n' "$_label" '<skipped>' "$(trunc "$_reason" 24)" "${C_DIM}SKIPPED${C_OFF}"
}

# all_resolutions <tool-basename> -> one absolute path per line, deduped.
# Walks $PATH by hand (not `type -a`, to keep the output format under this
# script's control) so a SECOND, hazardous resolution further down PATH is
# visible even when the first one works fine - "two python3s on one PATH" is
# literally what I-026 was.
all_resolutions() {
  _tool=$1
  _seen='|'
  _oldifs=$IFS
  set -f
  IFS=':'
  set -- $PATH
  IFS=$_oldifs
  set +f
  for _dir in "$@"; do
    [ -z "$_dir" ] && continue
    for _cand in "$_dir/$_tool" "$_dir/$_tool.exe"; do
      if [ -f "$_cand" ] && [ -x "$_cand" ]; then
        case "$_seen" in
          *"|$_cand|"*) : ;;
          *) printf '%s\n' "$_cand"; _seen="$_seen$_cand|" ;;
        esac
      fi
    done
  done
}

# install_hint <tool> -- a real, copy-paste command for THIS detected
# platform family. Windows entries are verified against what is actually
# installed on the certified host (see the tool-inventory group below);
# macOS/Linux entries are the standard command for that platform's usual
# package manager and are explicitly NOT verified - T-317 (blocked by
# D-019, still open) owns measuring those hosts for real.
install_hint() {
  case "$PLATFORM_FAMILY" in
    windows)
      case "$1" in
        jq)       echo 'winget install --id jqlang.jq -e' ;;
        python3)  echo 'winget install --id Python.Python.3.12 -e' ;;
        curl)     echo 'winget install --id cURL.cURL -e  (Windows 10 1803+ already ships curl.exe in System32)' ;;
        git)      echo 'winget install --id Git.Git -e  (also provides sed/tr/cut/sort/wc/find/tar/date/mktemp/dirname/basename/head/grep via its MSYS2 userland)' ;;
        composer) echo 'winget install --id Composer.Composer -e' ;;
        awk|grep|sed|tr|cut|sort|wc|find|tar|date|mktemp|dirname|basename|head)
                  echo "winget install --id Git.Git -e  (repairs the MSYS2 userland that ships $1; open a fresh terminal afterwards)" ;;
        docker)   echo 'winget install --id Docker.DockerDesktop -e' ;;
        ddev)     echo 'winget install --id drud.ddev -e' ;;
        *)        echo "no install command recorded for $1 on Windows" ;;
      esac
      ;;
    macos)
      case "$1" in
        jq)       echo 'brew install jq' ;;
        python3)  echo 'brew install python3' ;;
        curl)     echo 'brew install curl  (macOS already ships a system curl; only needed if that one is broken)' ;;
        git)      echo 'brew install git  (or: xcode-select --install)' ;;
        composer) echo 'brew install composer' ;;
        awk)      echo 'brew install gawk  (macOS ships one-true-awk; whether that matters here is what tests/bin/toolchain-floor measures - do not install it on a guess)' ;;
        grep|sed|tr|cut|sort|wc|find|tar|date|mktemp|dirname|basename|head)
                  echo "brew install coreutils  (macOS ships BSD $1 by default; whether GNU is actually required here is UNMEASURED - run tests/bin/toolchain-floor, T-803)" ;;
        docker)   echo 'brew install --cask docker' ;;
        ddev)     echo 'brew install ddev/ddev/ddev' ;;
        *)        echo "no install command recorded for $1 on macOS" ;;
      esac
      ;;
    linux)
      case "$1" in
        jq)       echo 'sudo apt-get install -y jq  (package name/manager varies by distro)' ;;
        python3)  echo 'sudo apt-get install -y python3' ;;
        curl)     echo 'sudo apt-get install -y curl' ;;
        git)      echo 'sudo apt-get install -y git' ;;
        composer) echo 'see https://getcomposer.org/download/  (package name/availability varies by distro)' ;;
        awk)      echo 'sudo apt-get install -y gawk  (or mawk - note mawk counts BYTES where gawk in a UTF-8 locale counts characters)' ;;
        grep|sed|tr|cut|sort|wc|find|tar|date|mktemp|dirname|basename|head)
                  echo 'sudo apt-get install -y coreutils findutils grep sed tar' ;;
        docker)   echo 'see https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/  (distro-specific)' ;;
        ddev)     echo 'see https://ddev.readthedocs.io/en/stable/users/install/ddev-installation/' ;;
        *)        echo "no install command recorded for $1 on Linux" ;;
      esac
      ;;
    *)
      echo "platform not recognised - no install command available (see Platform detection above)"
      ;;
  esac
}

# report_tool <tool> <label> <obtained> <expected> -> returns 0 if the tool
# is usable, 1 otherwise. Prints its exercise verdict, path, version and any
# extra PATH resolutions. The exercise command itself is run by the CALLER,
# not here, so what "a real, minimal invocation" means for each tool stays
# visible at the call site (I-026) instead of hidden in a generic loop.
report_tool() {
  _tool=$1; _label=$2; _got=$3; _exp=$4
  _path=$(command -v "$_tool" 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -z "$_path" ]; then
    check "$_label" '<not found>' "$_exp"
    TOOLS_MISSING=$((TOOLS_MISSING + 1))
    note "install: $(install_hint "$_tool")"
    return 1
  fi
  check "$_label" "$_got" "$_exp"
  # Strip ANSI colour escapes: composer's own --version banner is colourised
  # even when not attached to a real TTY, and a raw escape would either
  # print as literal bracket text or fight with this script's own C_DIM.
  _ver=$("$_tool" --version 2>&1 | head -1 | sed 's/\x1b\[[0-9;]*m//g')
  note "path:    $_path"
  note "version: ${_ver:-<no --version output>}"
  _all=$(all_resolutions "$_tool")
  if [ -n "$_all" ]; then _all_n=$(printf '%s\n' "$_all" | wc -l | tr -d ' '); else _all_n=0; fi
  if [ "$_all_n" -gt 1 ]; then
    note "PATH has $_all_n resolutions for '$_tool' (first one wins, but see below):"
    printf '%s\n' "$_all" | while IFS= read -r _p; do note "  - $_p"; done
    case "$_all" in
      *WindowsApps*) note "  ${C_WARN}one resolution is under WindowsApps - the Microsoft Store stub location (I-026).${C_OFF}" ;;
    esac
  fi
  if [ "$_got" != "$_exp" ]; then
    TOOLS_BROKEN=$((TOOLS_BROKEN + 1))
    note "fix: $(install_hint "$_tool")"
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'doctor - Agora - host readiness diagnostic (D-019 rider b) - NOT A GATE\n'
printf 'repo: %s\n' "$REPO_ROOT"
printf 'date: %s\n' "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ' 2>/dev/null || echo '<date unavailable>')"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

# ============================================================= 1. Platform --
group '1. Platform detection'
UNAME_S=$(uname -s 2>/dev/null); [ -z "${UNAME_S:-}" ] && UNAME_S='<unavailable>'
UNAME_M=$(uname -m 2>/dev/null); [ -z "${UNAME_M:-}" ] && UNAME_M='<unavailable>'
UNAME_R=$(uname -r 2>/dev/null)
HOST_NAME=$(hostname 2>/dev/null)

case "$UNAME_S" in
  MINGW*|MSYS*|CYGWIN*) PLATFORM_FAMILY=windows; PLATFORM_LABEL="Windows (MSYS2 / Git for Windows - $UNAME_S)" ;;
  Darwin*)              PLATFORM_FAMILY=macos;   PLATFORM_LABEL="macOS (Darwin $UNAME_R)" ;;
  Linux*)               PLATFORM_FAMILY=linux;   PLATFORM_LABEL="Linux ($UNAME_R)" ;;
  *)                     PLATFORM_FAMILY=unknown; PLATFORM_LABEL="unrecognised ($UNAME_S)" ;;
esac

note "uname -s -r:  $UNAME_S $UNAME_R"
note "uname -m:     $UNAME_M"
note "MSYSTEM:      ${MSYSTEM:-<not set>}"
note "hostname:     ${HOST_NAME:-<unknown>}"
note "shell:        bash ${BASH_VERSION:-<unknown>}"
note "platform family recognised as: $PLATFORM_LABEL"
if [ "$PLATFORM_FAMILY" = unknown ]; then
  printf '  %sWARNING:%s uname -s reports "%s", which this script does not recognise as\n' "$C_WARN" "$C_OFF" "$UNAME_S"
  printf '  Windows/macOS/Linux. Tool detection below still runs (it only needs PATH), but\n'
  printf '  install hints and certification status cannot be determined for it.\n'
fi

# Certification lookup against the CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS data block above.
CERT_STATUS=no
CERT_LABEL=$PLATFORM_LABEL
CERT_DATE='-'
CERT_TASK='-'
while IFS='|' read -r _fam _label _cert _date _task; do
  [ -z "$_fam" ] && continue
  if [ "$_fam" = "$PLATFORM_FAMILY" ]; then
    CERT_STATUS=$_cert; CERT_LABEL=$_label; CERT_DATE=$_date; CERT_TASK=$_task
  fi
done <<< "$CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS"

if [ "$CERT_STATUS" = yes ]; then
  printf '\n  %sCERTIFIED%s - dirty-case matrix run %s (%s). A green result here carries the\n' "$C_OK" "$C_OFF" "$CERT_DATE" "$CERT_TASK"
  printf '  same weight as a gate run in the container (D-019 rider c).\n'
else
  printf '\n  %sNOT CERTIFIED%s - no dirty-case matrix (T-312) has been run on this platform yet\n' "$C_WARN" "$C_OFF"
  printf '  (D-019 rider c). Host mode is still explicitly allowed here - this is a WARNING,\n'
  printf '  not a failure - but do not read a green invariant run on this platform as the\n'
  printf '  same guarantee as one from a certified platform or the container. See T-317.\n'
fi

# Full table, not just this platform's row: proof (not assertion) that no
# platform is claimed certified beyond what CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS actually
# says - readable on any host, not only the one currently running.
note ''
note 'full certification table (CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS, this file):'
while IFS='|' read -r _fam _label _cert _date _task; do
  [ -z "$_fam" ] && continue
  if [ "$_cert" = yes ]; then
    note "  - $_label: CERTIFIED ($_date, $_task)"
  else
    note "  - $_label: not certified"
  fi
done <<< "$CERTIFIED_PLATFORMS"

# ===================================================== 2. Required tools ---
group '2. Required tools (grep, jq, python3, curl, git, composer)'

# T-321(a) EXEMPTION, same reasoning as gate-a-wave3.sh: `grep -c` is the
# SUBJECT of this probe, not a counter feeding a guard, and report_tool compares
# it as a STRING against '1' - a blank fails loudly, never silently.
GREP_GOT=$(printf 'a' | grep -c a 2>&1)
if report_tool grep 'grep (printf a | grep -c a)' "$GREP_GOT" '1'; then GREP_OK=yes; else GREP_OK=no; fi

JQ_GOT=$(jq -n 1 2>&1)
if report_tool jq 'jq (jq -n 1)' "$JQ_GOT" '1'; then JQ_OK=yes; else JQ_OK=no; fi

PY3_GOT=$(python3 -c 'pass' >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?)
if report_tool python3 'python3 (python3 -c pass, exit)' "$PY3_GOT" '0'; then PY3_OK=yes; else PY3_OK=no; fi

CURL_GOT=$(curl --version >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?)
report_tool curl 'curl (curl --version, exit)' "$CURL_GOT" '0'

GIT_GOT=$(git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree 2>&1)
report_tool git 'git (rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree)' "$GIT_GOT" 'true'

COMPOSER_GOT=$(composer --version >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?)
report_tool composer 'composer (composer --version, exit)' "$COMPOSER_GOT" '0'

# ============================================ 3. Coreutils used by tests/bin --
group '3. Coreutils used by tests/bin/* (surveyed 2026-08-22, not guessed)'
note 'real invocations found (not comments/mentions) across no-boilerplate, no-ci-allow-dev,'
note 'no-code-in-template, no-patches, no-secrets, no-unstable-deps, sbom-check and both gate'
note 'runners. xargs is NOT used (it survives only in one comment).'
# T-802 spotted that the 2026-08-22 survey's claim about awk had gone stale and
# said so, but deliberately left awk without a probe: adding one changes this
# script's contract, and that belonged to whoever owned the toolchain floor.
# T-803 owns it, so the gap is closed here rather than described a second time.
# awk is now exercised like every other tool below, and how it COUNTS - the part
# a works/does-not-work probe cannot see - is a hazard in group 4.
note 'CLOSED 2026-08-25 (T-803): awk IS used - 7 invocations in config-inventory (T-601),'
note 'and it is now probed below, with its byte-vs-character behaviour in group 4.'

# Two fields and a length in one program: enough that a shell wrapper
# pretending to be awk, or an awk that cannot parse a rule with an action,
# fails here rather than four hundred lines into config-inventory.
AWK_GOT=$(printf 'x y\n' | awk '{print $2, length($1)}' 2>&1)
if report_tool awk 'awk (print $2, length($1) over "x y")' "$AWK_GOT" 'y 1'; then AWK_OK=yes; else AWK_OK=no; fi

SED_GOT=$(printf 'a' | sed 's/a/b/' 2>&1)
report_tool sed 'sed (printf a | sed s/a/b/)' "$SED_GOT" 'b'

TR_GOT=$(printf 'a' | tr 'a' 'b' 2>&1)
report_tool tr 'tr (printf a | tr a b)' "$TR_GOT" 'b'

CUT_GOT=$(printf 'a:b' | cut -d: -f2 2>&1)
report_tool cut 'cut (printf a:b | cut -d: -f2)' "$CUT_GOT" 'b'

SORT_GOT=$(printf 'b\na' | sort | tr '\n' ',' 2>&1)
report_tool sort 'sort (printf b\na | sort)' "$SORT_GOT" 'a,b,'

WC_GOT=$(printf 'a\nb\n' | wc -l | tr -d ' ' 2>&1)
report_tool wc 'wc (printf a\nb | wc -l)' "$WC_GOT" '2'

FIND_GOT=$(find . -maxdepth 0 >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?)
report_tool find 'find (find . -maxdepth 0, exit)' "$FIND_GOT" '0'

TAR_GOT=$(tar --version >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?)
report_tool tar 'tar (tar --version, exit)' "$TAR_GOT" '0'

DATE_GOT=$(date -u '+%Y' 2>&1 | grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}$' && echo 'yes' || echo 'no')
report_tool date 'date (date -u +%Y, is 4 digits)' "$DATE_GOT" 'yes'

MKTEMP_TMP=$(mktemp -t doctor.XXXXXX 2>&1)
if [ -f "$MKTEMP_TMP" ]; then MKTEMP_GOT='created'; rm -f "$MKTEMP_TMP"; else MKTEMP_GOT=$MKTEMP_TMP; fi
report_tool mktemp 'mktemp (creates a real file)' "$MKTEMP_GOT" 'created'

DIRNAME_GOT=$(dirname /a/b/c 2>&1)
report_tool dirname 'dirname (dirname /a/b/c)' "$DIRNAME_GOT" '/a/b'

BASENAME_GOT=$(basename /a/b/c 2>&1)
report_tool basename 'basename (basename /a/b/c)' "$BASENAME_GOT" 'c'

HEAD_GOT=$(printf 'a\nb\nc\n' | head -n 1 2>&1)
report_tool head 'head (printf a\nb\nc | head -n1)' "$HEAD_GOT" 'a'

# ================================================ 3b. The sha256 tool -----
# WHY THIS IS A GROUP AND NOT A report_tool CALL. report_tool counts a missing
# tool toward TOOLS_MISSING, which makes this script exit 1 and say NOT READY.
# A Mac with no `sha256sum` is not a broken host - it is a normal Mac, and
# `shasum` covers it. So the three implementations are reported, the selected
# one is NAMED, and none of that is a failure unless all three are gone.
#
# The point of naming it: drupal/agora_theme's shared-invariants picks the
# first of sha256sum / shasum / openssl that exists, and a drift detector that
# silently changed which program computes its hashes - between two hosts, or
# between a host and the gate - would be the I-054 shape exactly. If it is
# going to vary, the variation has to be visible, on both hosts, before it
# matters.
group '3b. sha256 (used by drupal/agora_theme shared-invariants, not by this repository)'
HASH_TOOL='<none>'
HASH_CMD=''
HASH_FOUND=0
# Same order shared-invariants itself resolves them in. Restating another
# script's logic is a risk, so it is stated as a fact to CHECK rather than one
# to trust: if that order ever changes this line is wrong, and the digests
# printed below are what let a reader notice.
for _h in sha256sum shasum openssl; do
  if command -v "$_h" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    HASH_FOUND=$((HASH_FOUND + 1))
    [ "$HASH_TOOL" = '<none>' ] && HASH_TOOL=$_h
  fi
done
case "$HASH_TOOL" in
  sha256sum) HASH_CMD='sha256sum' ;;
  shasum)    HASH_CMD='shasum -a 256' ;;
  openssl)   HASH_CMD='openssl dgst -sha256' ;;
esac
# The canary is the same five bytes shared-invariants hashes, and the digest
# whose first 16 characters gate-a-theme.sh asserts. A hasher that returned a
# constant would report every comparison in that script as equal, so it is
# EXERCISED here, not merely located.
HASH_CANARY_EXPECT='f7070d57bbe5496e29249421e91572f46ac4c2b62953b7ea046fa3707b9e6b2a'
case "$HASH_TOOL" in
  sha256sum) HASH_CANARY=$(printf 'agora' | sha256sum 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1) ;;
  shasum)    HASH_CANARY=$(printf 'agora' | shasum -a 256 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1) ;;
  openssl)   HASH_CANARY=$(printf 'agora' | openssl dgst -sha256 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^.*= *//') ;;
  *)         HASH_CANARY='' ;;
esac
if [ "$HASH_FOUND" -eq 0 ]; then
  HASH_SUMMARY='NONE - sha256sum, shasum and openssl are all absent'
  note "sha256 tool: ${C_BAD}none found${C_OFF} (looked for sha256sum, shasum, openssl)"
  note 'This repository does not need one. drupal/agora_theme does: its'
  note 'shared-invariants is built entirely out of sha256 comparisons and will'
  note 'refuse to run here, loudly, rather than report a false clean.'
else
  note "sha256 implementations present: $HASH_FOUND of 3 (sha256sum, shasum, openssl)"
  for _h in sha256sum shasum openssl; do
    _p=$(command -v "$_h" 2>/dev/null)
    note "  $_h: ${_p:-ABSENT}"
  done
  check 'sha256 selected implementation' "$HASH_CMD" "$HASH_CMD"
  if [ "$HASH_CANARY" = "$HASH_CANARY_EXPECT" ]; then
    HASH_SUMMARY="$HASH_CMD (canary OK)"
    check "sha256 canary: first 16 of sha256('agora')" "$(printf '%s' "$HASH_CANARY" | cut -c1-16)" 'f7070d57bbe5496e'
  else
    HASH_SUMMARY="$HASH_CMD - CANARY FAILED"
    check "sha256 canary: first 16 of sha256('agora')" "${HASH_CANARY:-<no output>}" 'f7070d57bbe5496e'
    TOOLS_BROKEN=$((TOOLS_BROKEN + 1))
    note 'A hash tool that cannot reproduce a known digest cannot detect drift either.'
  fi
  if [ "$HASH_TOOL" != sha256sum ]; then
    note "${C_WARN}NOTE:${C_OFF} no sha256sum on this host - falling back to '$HASH_CMD'."
    note 'Expected on macOS, and not a defect: the digests are identical, falsified'
    note 'under a PATH shim in T-803. It is printed because a drift detector quietly'
    note 'changing which program computes its hashes is the silent-input-change shape'
    note 'of I-054, and the fix for that is noise, not confidence.'
  fi
fi

# ================================================= 4. Known-hazard probes ---
printf '\n%s\n' '4. Known-hazard probes (reproduced live on this host, not assumed - I-025/I-026/I-027)'
printf '  %-46s %-24s | %-24s | %s\n' 'probe' 'source' 'consequence if present' 'status'
printf '  %s\n' "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------"

if [ "$GREP_OK" = yes ]; then
  GREP_HAZ_OUT=$(printf 'hello\n' | grep -IFin 'hello' 2>&1)
  GREP_HAZ_RC=$?
  if [ "$GREP_HAZ_RC" -ge 128 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    hazard 'grep -IFin abort (-F + -i together)' PRESENT "rc=$GREP_HAZ_RC (SIGABRT) - deny-list scans like no-boilerplate become silent no-ops" 'I-027'
  else
    hazard 'grep -IFin abort (-F + -i together)' ABSENT "rc=$GREP_HAZ_RC - this grep does not abort on -IFin" 'I-027'
  fi
else
  hazard_skipped 'grep -IFin abort (-F + -i together)' 'grep itself is not usable, see group 2 above'
fi

if [ "$JQ_OK" = yes ]; then
  JQ_MULTI=$(jq -rn '"line1","line2","line3"' 2>/dev/null)
  case "$JQ_MULTI" in
    *$'\r'*) hazard 'jq CRLF on stdout (multi-line output)' PRESENT 'every line but the last gains a stray CR, desyncing string compares' 'I-025' ;;
    *)       hazard 'jq CRLF on stdout (multi-line output)' ABSENT  'n/a - stdout is clean LF' 'I-025' ;;
  esac
else
  hazard_skipped 'jq CRLF on stdout (multi-line output)' 'jq itself is not usable, see group 2 above'
fi

if [ "$PY3_OK" = yes ]; then
  PY_MULTI=$(python3 -c "print('line1'); print('line2'); print('line3')" 2>/dev/null)
  case "$PY_MULTI" in
    *$'\r'*) hazard 'python3 CRLF on stdout (multi-line output)' PRESENT 'same corruption as jq, same fix (tr -d \r at the boundary)' 'I-025' ;;
    *)       hazard 'python3 CRLF on stdout (multi-line output)' ABSENT  'n/a - stdout is clean LF' 'I-025' ;;
  esac

  UTF8_PROBE_SRC='
import locale
enc = locale.getpreferredencoding(False)
try:
    with open("recipe.yml", encoding=enc) as f:
        f.read()
    print("ok:" + enc)
except UnicodeDecodeError:
    print("decodeerror:" + enc)
except FileNotFoundError:
    print("notfound")
except Exception as e:
    print("othererror:" + type(e).__name__)
'
  UTF8_PROBE_OUT=$(python3 -c "$UTF8_PROBE_SRC" 2>&1)
  case "$UTF8_PROBE_OUT" in
    decodeerror:*) hazard 'python3 default encoding vs recipe.yml' PRESENT "preferred encoding is ${UTF8_PROBE_OUT#decodeerror:} - dies on the A-acute in 'Agora' (T-320c)" 'T-320c' ;;
    ok:*)          hazard 'python3 default encoding vs recipe.yml' ABSENT  "reads clean with ${UTF8_PROBE_OUT#ok:}" 'T-320c' ;;
    notfound)      hazard_skipped 'python3 default encoding vs recipe.yml' 'recipe.yml not found from repo root' ;;
    *)             hazard_skipped 'python3 default encoding vs recipe.yml' "$UTF8_PROBE_OUT" ;;
  esac
else
  hazard_skipped 'python3 CRLF on stdout (multi-line output)' 'python3 itself is not usable, see group 2 above'
  hazard_skipped 'python3 default encoding vs recipe.yml' 'python3 itself is not usable, see group 2 above'
fi

# awk counting BYTES where a character-based rule counts CHARACTERS. Found by
# T-802 in the gate container, whose awk is mawk; found again by T-803 on this
# host, where gawk counts bytes under a C locale and characters under a UTF-8
# one - the SAME binary, two answers, decided by an environment variable. Both
# are therefore measured; neither is inferred from the other. Nothing in
# tests/bin/ depends on this today - config-inventory uses awk to match and
# count lines only - so this is a hazard, not a failure. It is here so that the
# next character-based rule written in awk is written knowingly.
if [ "$AWK_OK" = yes ]; then
  # 'a' + U+00E1 + 'b': three characters, four bytes. Octal escapes, so this
  # file's own encoding cannot change what is being measured.
  AWK_LEN=$(printf 'a\303\241b\n' | awk '{print length}' 2>/dev/null)
  AWK_LEN_UTF8=$(printf 'a\303\241b\n' | LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 awk '{print length}' 2>/dev/null)
  [ -z "${AWK_LEN_UTF8:-}" ] && AWK_LEN_UTF8=$(printf 'a\303\241b\n' | LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 awk '{print length}' 2>/dev/null)
  if [ "${AWK_LEN:-}" = 4 ]; then
    hazard 'awk length counts bytes, not characters' PRESENT "length=4 for a 3-character string with LANG=${LANG:-unset}; a forced UTF-8 locale gives ${AWK_LEN_UTF8:-?}. Never use awk for a character-based rule without pinning the locale" 'T-802/T-803'
  elif [ "${AWK_LEN:-}" = 3 ]; then
    hazard 'awk length counts bytes, not characters' ABSENT "length=3 - this awk counts characters in the ambient locale (LANG=${LANG:-unset})" 'T-802/T-803'
  else
    hazard_skipped 'awk length counts bytes, not characters' "awk returned [${AWK_LEN:-<blank>}] for a 3-character string - neither 3 nor 4"
  fi
else
  hazard_skipped 'awk length counts bytes, not characters' 'awk itself is not usable, see group 3 above'
fi

# ============================================== 5. Container availability --
group '5. Container availability (Docker / DDEV - the D-019-certified path)'
DOCKER_PATH=$(command -v docker 2>/dev/null)
DOCKER_DAEMON='n/a (no CLI)'
if [ -n "$DOCKER_PATH" ]; then
  DOCKER_VER=$(docker --version 2>&1)
  note "docker CLI:    $DOCKER_PATH ($DOCKER_VER)"
  DOCKER_ERR_FILE=$(mktemp -t doctor-docker.XXXXXX 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -n "$DOCKER_ERR_FILE" ] && docker info >/dev/null 2>"$DOCKER_ERR_FILE"; then
    DOCKER_DAEMON=reachable
  else
    if [ -n "${DOCKER_ERR_FILE:-}" ] && [ -f "$DOCKER_ERR_FILE" ]; then
      DOCKER_ERR_MSG=$(tr -d '\r' < "$DOCKER_ERR_FILE" | tr '\n' ' ' | cut -c1-100)
    else
      DOCKER_ERR_MSG=''
    fi
    DOCKER_DAEMON="NOT reachable (${DOCKER_ERR_MSG:-daemon did not respond})"
  fi
  [ -n "${DOCKER_ERR_FILE:-}" ] && rm -f "$DOCKER_ERR_FILE" 2>/dev/null
  note "docker daemon: $DOCKER_DAEMON"
else
  note "docker CLI:    not found - $(install_hint docker)"
fi

DDEV_PATH=$(command -v ddev 2>/dev/null)
if [ -n "$DDEV_PATH" ]; then
  DDEV_VER=$(ddev --version 2>&1)
  note "ddev CLI:      $DDEV_PATH ($DDEV_VER)"
else
  note "ddev CLI:      not found - $(install_hint ddev)"
fi

# ---- WSL probe (I-046) -----------------------------------------------
# A capability absent from the installation just checked above is not a
# capability absent from the machine. DDEV's OWN recommended Windows setup
# is docker-ce running INSIDE WSL2 - a different, valid path, not a
# degraded one - and it conflicts with Docker Desktop's named pipe. Only
# probe here, so Linux/macOS and the already-reachable-on-Windows case stay
# exactly as fast as before: nothing below runs unless the Windows-side
# daemon just came back unreachable AND this is a Windows host.
WSL_STATE=n/a
if [ "$PLATFORM_FAMILY" = windows ] && [ "$DOCKER_DAEMON" != reachable ]; then
  note ''
  note 'Windows-side docker daemon not reachable - probing WSL before concluding anything (I-046):'
  WSL_EXE=$(command -v wsl.exe 2>/dev/null)
  [ -z "$WSL_EXE" ] && WSL_EXE=$(command -v wsl 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -z "$WSL_EXE" ]; then
    WSL_STATE=no_wsl
    note '  wsl.exe: not found on PATH - no WSL on this host (or it is not installed). Nothing more to probe.'
  else
    note "  wsl.exe: $WSL_EXE"
    # `wsl -l -v` prints UTF-16 with embedded NULs on a Windows console
    # (I-025's family) - strip NULs (and any CR) INSIDE the same pipeline
    # that captures it, before bash's own command substitution ever sees a
    # NUL byte (which it would otherwise warn about on stderr), or the
    # parsing silently produces nonsense.
    WSL_LIST=$("$WSL_EXE" -l -v 2>&1 | tr -d '\0' | tr -d '\r')
    WSL_DISTRO_LINES=$(printf '%s\n' "$WSL_LIST" | grep -E 'Running|Stopped' 2>/dev/null)
    WSL_GREP_RC=$?
    if [ "$WSL_GREP_RC" -ge 2 ]; then
      note "  wsl.exe -l -v: grep failed parsing the distro list (rc=$WSL_GREP_RC) - raw output:"
      note "    $(printf '%s' "$WSL_LIST" | tr '\n' ' ' | cut -c1-150)"
      WSL_DISTRO_LINES=''
    fi
    if [ -z "$WSL_DISTRO_LINES" ]; then
      WSL_DISTRO_COUNT=0
    else
      WSL_DISTRO_COUNT=$(printf '%s\n' "$WSL_DISTRO_LINES" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
    fi
    note "  distros found: ${WSL_DISTRO_COUNT:-0}"
    if [ "${WSL_DISTRO_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
      WSL_STATE=no_distros
      note '  no WSL distros are installed - nothing to run docker or ddev inside.'
    else
      printf '%s\n' "$WSL_DISTRO_LINES" | while IFS= read -r _dl; do note "    $_dl"; done
      WSL_DEFAULT_LINE=$(printf '%s\n' "$WSL_DISTRO_LINES" | grep '^\*' 2>/dev/null)
      WSL_DEFAULT_NAME=$(printf '%s' "$WSL_DEFAULT_LINE" | sed -E 's/^\*? *//' | tr -s ' ' | cut -d' ' -f1)
      [ -z "$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME" ] && WSL_DEFAULT_NAME='<no default set>'
      WSL_DD_LINE=$(printf '%s\n' "$WSL_DISTRO_LINES" | grep 'docker-desktop' 2>/dev/null | grep -v 'docker-desktop-data' 2>/dev/null)
      WSL_DD_STATE=''
      if [ -n "$WSL_DD_LINE" ]; then
        WSL_DD_STATE=$(printf '%s' "$WSL_DD_LINE" | sed -E 's/^\*? *//' | tr -s ' ' | cut -d' ' -f2)
      fi

      note "  probing default distro '$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME' for a docker daemon..."
      WSL_DOCKER_OUT=$("$WSL_EXE" -e bash -lc 'docker info --format "{{.ServerVersion}}"' 2>&1)
      WSL_DOCKER_RC=$?
      WSL_DOCKER_OUT=$(printf '%s' "$WSL_DOCKER_OUT" | tr -d '\0' | tr -d '\r')

      if [ "$WSL_DOCKER_RC" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$WSL_DOCKER_OUT" ]; then
        note "  docker inside WSL:  reachable (server $WSL_DOCKER_OUT, distro '$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME')"
        WSL_DDEV_OUT=$("$WSL_EXE" -e bash -lc 'command -v ddev && ddev version' 2>&1)
        WSL_DDEV_RC=$?
        WSL_DDEV_OUT=$(printf '%s' "$WSL_DDEV_OUT" | tr -d '\0' | tr -d '\r')
        if [ "$WSL_DDEV_RC" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$WSL_DDEV_OUT" ]; then
          WSL_DDEV_STATUS="ddev present ($(printf '%s' "$WSL_DDEV_OUT" | tr '\n' ' ' | cut -c1-80))"
        else
          WSL_DDEV_STATUS='ddev not found inside this distro'
        fi
        note "  ddev inside WSL:    $WSL_DDEV_STATUS"

        if [ -n "$WSL_DD_LINE" ] && [ "$WSL_DD_STATE" != Running ]; then
          WSL_STATE=docker_ok_dd_stopped
          note "  ${C_WARN}NOTE:${C_OFF} a docker-desktop WSL distro exists and is $WSL_DD_STATE, while distro"
          note "  '$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME' has a working daemon of its own - this is the exact shape"
          note "  that produced the false 'daemon unreachable' report in I-046. The stopped"
          note "  docker-desktop distro belongs to Docker Desktop, a different product from the"
          note "  one that is actually running - leave it stopped, it is not the problem."
        else
          WSL_STATE=docker_ok
        fi
        note "  this IS DDEV's own recommended Windows setup, not a degraded one - do not"
        note "  install Docker Desktop to 'fix' this; the two conflict."
        note "  run project work there, e.g.: wsl.exe -e bash -lc 'cd ~/agora-smoke && ddev ...'"
        note "  the project must live IN the WSL filesystem (~/project) - never under /mnt/c,"
        note "  which DDEV documents as slow and permission-prone."
      else
        WSL_STATE=no_daemon
        note "  docker inside WSL:  NOT reachable in default distro '$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME' either"
        note "    ($(printf '%s' "$WSL_DOCKER_OUT" | tr '\n' ' ' | cut -c1-100))"
      fi
    fi
  fi
fi

if [ -n "$DOCKER_PATH" ] && [ "$DOCKER_DAEMON" = reachable ]; then
  CONTAINER_SUMMARY='available (docker daemon reachable on Windows)'
elif [ "$WSL_STATE" = docker_ok ]; then
  CONTAINER_SUMMARY="available in WSL distro '$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME' (server $WSL_DOCKER_OUT) - DDEV's recommended Windows setup, not degraded; work inside WSL (~/project), not /mnt/c"
elif [ "$WSL_STATE" = docker_ok_dd_stopped ]; then
  CONTAINER_SUMMARY="available in WSL distro '$WSL_DEFAULT_NAME' (server $WSL_DOCKER_OUT) - a separate, stopped docker-desktop distro is expected and NOT the problem (I-046)"
elif [ -n "$DOCKER_PATH" ]; then
  CONTAINER_SUMMARY='CLI present, daemon unreachable on Windows, and no working daemon found in WSL either - start Docker Desktop, or set up docker-ce inside WSL2 (see notes above), and re-run'
else
  CONTAINER_SUMMARY='unavailable - no docker CLI on PATH, and no working daemon found in WSL either'
fi
note "verdict: $CONTAINER_SUMMARY"
note '(informational only - D-019 rider (c) keeps host mode valid with no container at all)'

# ---- the pin (T-802) -------------------------------------------------
# D-019 put the gate's toolchain in a container; T-802 requires that
# container to be named by sha256 DIGEST and requires this script to
# REPORT that digest. A digest stored but never shown is not reported: the
# whole failure mode being guarded here is an environment that moves while
# calling itself pinned, and nobody notices because nobody is ever told
# what it is running.
#
# Read out of tests/container/compose.yaml rather than restated. Two
# scripts naming the same image is exactly the drift this is meant to
# prevent, so there is one source and everything else reads it.
#
# `tr -d '\r'` first: on a Windows checkout compose.yaml is CRLF
# (core.autocrlf is set at system scope by the Git for Windows installer,
# and .gitattributes pins only tests/bin/** and *.sh to LF), and without
# this the digest would be printed with a stray carriage return in it.
note ''
GATE_COMPOSE='tests/container/compose.yaml'
GATE_IMAGE_REF=''
GATE_DIGEST=''
if [ -f "$GATE_COMPOSE" ]; then
  GATE_IMAGE_REF=$(tr -d '\r' < "$GATE_COMPOSE" | sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*image:[[:space:]]*//p' | head -1)
fi
if [ -z "$GATE_IMAGE_REF" ]; then
  GATE_PIN_SUMMARY="NO PIN FOUND - $GATE_COMPOSE is missing or names no image (T-802)"
  note "gate container pin: ${C_WARN}not found${C_OFF} in $GATE_COMPOSE"
else
  GATE_IMAGE_NAME=${GATE_IMAGE_REF%@*}
  case "$GATE_IMAGE_REF" in
    *@sha256:*)
      GATE_DIGEST=${GATE_IMAGE_REF##*@}
      note "gate container image:  $GATE_IMAGE_NAME"
      note "gate container digest: $GATE_DIGEST"
      GATE_PIN_SUMMARY="$GATE_DIGEST"
      ;;
    *)
      # A tag where a digest was promised. Reported as a WARNING and named
      # for what it is, never quietly accepted: `:production` today is not
      # the same bytes as `:production` next month.
      note "gate container image:  $GATE_IMAGE_REF"
      note "gate container digest: ${C_BAD}NONE - that is a TAG, not a sha256 digest${C_OFF} (T-802)"
      GATE_PIN_SUMMARY="NOT PINNED - $GATE_COMPOSE names a tag, not a digest (T-802)"
      ;;
  esac
fi

# Is the pinned image actually on this machine, and is it these bytes? Only
# asked when a daemon was found above; a missing image is not a problem, it
# is a `docker pull` away, so this reports rather than judges.
if [ -n "$GATE_DIGEST" ]; then
  GATE_LOCAL_OUT=''
  GATE_LOCAL_RC=1
  if [ -n "$DOCKER_PATH" ] && [ "$DOCKER_DAEMON" = reachable ]; then
    GATE_LOCAL_OUT=$(docker image inspect "$GATE_IMAGE_REF" --format '{{.Id}}' 2>&1)
    GATE_LOCAL_RC=$?
  elif [ -n "${WSL_EXE:-}" ] && { [ "$WSL_STATE" = docker_ok ] || [ "$WSL_STATE" = docker_ok_dd_stopped ]; }; then
    GATE_LOCAL_OUT=$("$WSL_EXE" -e bash -lc "docker image inspect '$GATE_IMAGE_REF' --format '{{.Id}}'" 2>&1)
    GATE_LOCAL_RC=$?
    GATE_LOCAL_OUT=$(printf '%s' "$GATE_LOCAL_OUT" | tr -d '\0' | tr -d '\r')
  fi
  if [ "$GATE_LOCAL_RC" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$GATE_LOCAL_OUT" ]; then
    note "gate container local:  present ($(printf '%s' "$GATE_LOCAL_OUT" | cut -c1-19)...)"
  elif [ -n "$GATE_LOCAL_OUT" ]; then
    note 'gate container local:  not pulled yet - run:'
    note "  docker pull $GATE_IMAGE_REF"
  else
    note 'gate container local:  not checked (no reachable daemon to ask)'
  fi
  note "run the gate in it with: tests/bin/gate-in-container"
fi

# ============================================== 6. Checkouts, clones and rigs --
# ADDED 2026-08-26, and it exists because CLAUDE.md:161-162 said this script
# "prints both real paths when you need them, and its output beats this file" -
# and it printed ONE path. Two separate readers found the same divergence on the
# same day. An instruction to trust doctor over the file is only sound while
# doctor actually answers the question, so it answers it here.
#
# THE REASON THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. On 2026-08-26 a rebuild found that
# ~/agora-smoke/source - a FULL CLONE of the site template - still carried a
# remote named `origin` with a LIVE PUSH URL, while CLAUDE.md stated that both
# WSL clones had been renamed and had their push disabled on 2026-08-24. It was
# true of one of them. In that rig a bare `git push` would have SUCCEEDED against
# the canonical repository - which is the exact accident the deliberate absence of
# an `origin` remote in the real working copy exists to prevent.
#
# So this group does not ask a file what the state is. It walks the disk, finds
# every git checkout that could be mistaken for a working copy, and prints for
# each one whether its push URL is live. Nothing here is a gate: it is a
# diagnostic, and its whole value is that it is MEASURED at the moment it is read.
group '6. Checkouts, clones and rigs (measured now, not read from a file)'

# The two real working copies. The theme is a SIBLING of the template, never a
# subdirectory: RequirementsTest requires zero *.info.yml files in the packaged
# site template, and a theme checkout nested anywhere under it is one `git add -A`
# away from making that permanently false.
note "template working copy: $REPO_ROOT"
DOCTOR_THEME_DIR=$(cd "$REPO_ROOT/../agora-theme" 2>/dev/null && { pwd -W 2>/dev/null || pwd; })
if [ -n "${DOCTOR_THEME_DIR:-}" ] && [ -d "$DOCTOR_THEME_DIR/.git" ]; then
  note "theme working copy:    $DOCTOR_THEME_DIR"
else
  note 'theme working copy:    NOT FOUND beside the template - expected a sibling named agora-theme'
fi

for _d in "$REPO_ROOT" "${DOCTOR_THEME_DIR:-}"; do
  [ -n "$_d" ] && [ -d "$_d/.git" ] || continue
  _push=$(git -C "$_d" remote -v 2>/dev/null | awk '$3=="(push)"{printf "%s=%s ", $1, $2}')
  [ -z "$_push" ] && _push='<none>'
  note "  remotes (push):      $_push"
  if git -C "$_d" remote 2>/dev/null | grep -qx origin; then
    note '  WARNING: a remote named `origin` exists here. The working copies deliberately have none,'
    note '           so that a bare `git push` fails loudly instead of reaching a remote by accident.'
  fi
done

# Every WSL rig, and whether it hides a clone. A rig is disposable; a clone
# inside a rig is the thing that looks like a working copy and is not one.
DOCTOR_WSL=$(command -v wsl.exe 2>/dev/null || command -v wsl 2>/dev/null)
if [ -n "${DOCTOR_WSL:-}" ]; then
  DOCTOR_RIGS=$(wsl.exe -e bash -lc 'for d in ~/agora-*; do [ -d "$d" ] || continue; if [ -d "$d/source/.git" ]; then p=$(git -C "$d/source" remote -v 2>/dev/null | awk "\$3==\"(push)\"{printf \"%s=%s \", \$1, \$2}"); [ -z "$p" ] && p="<none>"; echo "$d|CLONE|$p"; else echo "$d|rig only|-"; fi; done' 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\r')
  if [ -n "$DOCTOR_RIGS" ]; then
    RIG_TOTAL=0; RIG_CLONES=0; RIG_LIVE=0
    printf '%s\n' "$DOCTOR_RIGS" | while IFS='|' read -r _p _k _r; do
      [ -n "$_p" ] || continue
      note "$(printf '%-28s %-9s %s' "$_p" "$_k" "$_r")"
    done
    RIG_TOTAL=$(printf '%s\n' "$DOCTOR_RIGS" | grep -c .)
    RIG_CLONES=$(printf '%s\n' "$DOCTOR_RIGS" | grep -c '|CLONE|')
    RIG_LIVE=$(printf '%s\n' "$DOCTOR_RIGS" | grep -c '|CLONE|.*=https')
    note "rigs: $RIG_TOTAL - of which hold a full clone: $RIG_CLONES - clones whose push URL is LIVE: $RIG_LIVE"
    if [ "${RIG_LIVE:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
      note 'WARNING: a clone inside a throwaway rig can push to the canonical repository.'
      note '         Disable it:  git -C <rig>/source remote set-url --push <name> DISABLED-throwaway-rig'
      note '         `git pull` keeps working, which is all a rig ever needs.'
    fi
    note 'None of these is ever edited or committed from. They are rebuilt, never treated as a checkout.'
  else
    note 'no ~/agora-* rigs found in the WSL distro (or the query failed)'
  fi
else
  note 'wsl.exe not on PATH - rig inventory not taken (this is a Windows-only concern)'
fi

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- summary --
TOOLS_UNUSABLE=$((TOOLS_MISSING + TOOLS_BROKEN))

printf '\n=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'DOCTOR SUMMARY -- this is a DIAGNOSTIC, not a gate; gate verdicts live in\n'
printf 'tests/bin/gate-a-wave1.sh and tests/bin/gate-a-wave3.sh. Do not quote this as one.\n'
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf '  platform:        %s\n' "$PLATFORM_LABEL"
printf '  tools missing:   %d\n' "$TOOLS_MISSING"
printf '  tools broken:    %d\n' "$TOOLS_BROKEN"
printf '  hazards probed:  %d (%d present - informational, already handled at their call sites)\n' "$HAZ_TOTAL" "$HAZ_PRESENT"
printf '  container:       %s\n' "$CONTAINER_SUMMARY"
printf '  gate image pin:  %s\n' "$GATE_PIN_SUMMARY"
printf '  sha256 tool:     %s\n' "$HASH_SUMMARY"
if [ "$CERT_STATUS" = yes ]; then
  printf '  certification:   %sCERTIFIED%s (matrix run %s, %s)\n' "$C_OK" "$C_OFF" "$CERT_DATE" "$CERT_TASK"
else
  printf '  certification:   %sNOT CERTIFIED%s (D-019 rider c - see group 1 above)\n' "$C_WARN" "$C_OFF"
fi
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

if [ "$TOOLS_UNUSABLE" -gt 0 ]; then
  printf '%sNOT READY%s - %d required tool(s) unusable on this host. Fix them (see above) and re-run.\n' \
    "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$TOOLS_UNUSABLE"
  exit 1
fi

printf '%sREADY%s - every required tool is usable on this host.\n' "$C_OK" "$C_OFF"
if [ "$CERT_STATUS" != yes ]; then
  printf '%sNOT CERTIFIED%s - proceed in host mode at your own risk; this is not the same guarantee\n' "$C_WARN" "$C_OFF"
  printf 'as a green result from a certified platform or the container. Prefer the container when in doubt.\n'
fi
exit 0
